course: everyday
lesson: 361
title: Spoiling and losing things
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Ollie in Glasgow tore pages out of his maths exercise book to make paper planes. For a fortnight the teacher did not notice — until the book fell apart in her hands. She wrote a note in his planner, and Mum and Dad had to sign it. At home there was a row, the console went in the cupboard for a week, and Ollie slammed his door shouting «it's only paper!» By Sunday the sulk had worn off. He said sorry, paid for a new book from his pocket money and copied his work neatly into it.
explain: Isla left her bike unlocked outside the shop «for one minute», and it was stolen. Nobody punished her — losing the bike was the punishment. Dad bought a second-hand one, and Isla paid part of it and now locks it every time.
explain: Spoiling things on purpose in anger, or losing them by carelessness, costs money, time and sometimes someone else's work. The bad mark or the lost console is not revenge from grown-ups; it is the cost of what happened, made clear.
explain: Feeling cross and hard-done-by after a punishment is normal. It fades — and putting things right is still your job.
fact: Breaking your own things in anger leaves you without them when you calm down.
fact: A fridge door left open lets the inside warm up, and food like milk can go off.
fact: Buying things on impulse often means having no money for the things you really want.
fact: Exercise books hold your work; tearing pages out loses that work.
fact: A bath left running can overflow and damage floors and the ceiling below.
fact: Throwing food on the floor wastes it, and someone has to clean it up.
fact: Spoiling someone else's picture or work hurts them as well as the thing.
fact: An unlocked bike is much easier to steal than a locked one.
fact: A punishment such as losing a console is a consequence, not revenge.
fact: Sulking after a fair consequence is understandable, but it does not put things right.
exercise: Why did Ollie tear pages out of his exercise book? | To make paper planes for fun | The teacher asked him to do it | The pages were blank and spare | To give his friends some paper
exercise: For two weeks nobody noticed. What does that show? | Getting away with it for a while does not make it fine | Exercise books never really get checked by anybody | Tearing pages is allowed | The teacher did not care
exercise: What were the consequences when it came out? | A planner note, a row and the console put away | A prize for paper planes | Nothing happened, because it was only some paper | A new book bought by the school
exercise: Ollie shouted «it's only paper!» What is closer to the truth? | The pages held his school work | Paper has no value at all, so it doesn't matter | Exercise books are for planes | The teacher should buy more
exercise: What put things right after Ollie's sulk? | Saying sorry, paying for a new book and copying his work | Sulking in his room until the console came back | Saying the teacher lost the pages | Making planes from a different book
exercise: Mia broke her crayons on purpose because she was cross. What happened next? | She had no crayons to finish her picture | Mum bought her a new box of crayons straight away | Her crayons fixed themselves | Her sister lent her crayons forever
exercise: Ben left the fridge door open. What went wrong? | The milk went off and food was thrown away | The fridge got even colder and the milk froze | The food stayed fresh longer | The light bulb broke
exercise: Grace spent her pocket money on a toy she did not really want. What did she learn? | Think before you spend | Spend it faster next time | Toys are always a waste | Never buy anything at all
exercise: Leo left the bath running and forgot it. What happened? | It overflowed and Mum had to clean up | It drained away all by itself, so nothing happened | The water stayed just right | The bath turned itself off
exercise: What should Leo do next time he runs a bath? | Stay with it until it's ready | Run it much hotter so it fills up quickly | Run it faster | Leave the door shut
exercise: Ella threw her dinner on the floor because she did not like it. What was the consequence? | She had to clean up the mess herself | She got a different dinner that she liked at once | The dog ate it and that was fine | She got extra pudding
exercise: Sam scribbled on his brother's picture in anger. What put it right? | Saying sorry and helping draw a new one | Saying the picture was ugly anyway, so it didn't matter | Scribbling on his own picture too | Hiding the picture
exercise: Isla's bike was stolen when she left it unlocked. Who punished her? | Nobody; losing the bike was the cost | The police came round and gave her a fine | Her teacher | The shopkeeper
exercise: How did Isla make up for the lost bike? | She paid part of the next one and locks it now | She took her brother's bike | She said bikes always get stolen, so it was not her fault | She stopped riding forever
exercise: Ollie said «Mum took the console just to get back at me». What is closer to the truth? | It was a consequence of what he did, not revenge | Mum just wanted to play on the console herself | It was a random punishment | Consoles are taken every month
exercise: Why does Ollie's sulk not put things right? | The book is still torn until he fixes it | Sulking for long enough mends the book | Sulking makes the teacher forget | Sulking earns the console back
test: «only leaving my bike for 1 min, no one's gonna nick it». What's wise? | Lock it, even for a minute | It's fine to leave it for one minute | Leave it by the road, it's fine | Leave the key in the lock for later
test: Mum: «Who left the fridge open? The milk's off!» It was you. Best reply? | «Me, sorry, I'll close it properly» | «The fridge always does that by itself» | «The milk was old anyway» | «Ask Dad»
test: «so cross i snapped all my crayons lol». What happens next? | You have no crayons to draw with | They grow back | Mum buys you new ones straight away | They draw better broken
test: «it's ONLY paper, why's everyone so mad». The pages had your maths on. Which is true? | Your work was torn out, not just paper | Paper is free, so tearing it costs nothing | Nobody needs maths books | Teachers like planes
test: tore pages from my book last term and nobody noticed, «it worked last time, so it's fine». Is it? | No, you were lucky, and the work was still lost | Yes, if nobody noticed last term then it's fine | Yes, books have spare pages | Only in maths books
test: «mum took my xbox for a WEEK, she's so unfair». You'd torn up your book. Which is true? | It's the consequence of what you did, not unfairness | She's just being unfair to you for no reason at all | She wanted the Xbox | It's random
test: bath's running, you go to check your phone «for a sec». What should you do? | Stay with the bath | Check the phone for ten minutes | Turn the taps up full | Go downstairs and wait
test: bro: «you scribbled on my picture you idiot!» You did, when you were cross. Best move? | Say sorry and help him draw a new one | Say it looks much better now with your scribbles | Call him an idiot back | Tear it up so it's gone
test: «£5, saw a toy, bought it, it's rubbish, now I can't get the comic». Lesson? | Think before you spend | Always buy the first thing | Comics are always a waste | Money is only for toys and sweets
test: You've stopped sulking after the console row. What's the next step? | Say sorry and put right the damage | Wait in your room for Mum to say sorry first | Pretend it never happened | Sulk a bit longer to be sure
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the withdrawal of a privilege following damage to school property is not to be regarded as revenge. | It is a consequence proportionate to the conduct and not an act of retaliation | It is undertaken chiefly for the benefit and convenience of the parent concerned | It is imposed at random | It has no connection with the conduct
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the leaving of a bicycle unlocked in a public place? | It greatly increases the likelihood of theft | It is entirely safe provided that the period is under one minute | It deters thieves | It has no effect upon the risk of theft
exam: It is given that a child, having been punished, sulked and later ceased to sulk. Select the step most consistent with making amends. | The child apologises and repairs or replaces what was spoiled | The child awaits an apology from the parent before taking any further step | The child disregards the matter | The child resumes sulking
exam: State the consequence most commonly associated with the destruction of one's own possessions in anger. | The person is deprived of them once calm returns | The possessions are replaced by the parent without any cost to the child | The anger is permanently cured | No consequence follows
